---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/drip-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/drip-alternative');
const desc = 'Decide whether to repair a Drip workflow or move it by mapping branches, events, tags and queued messages before changing senders.';
const faqs = [{'question': 'When should we stay with Drip?', 'answer': 'Stay when repaired events, exclusions and templates satisfy the requirements and operators can maintain the flow. Native SMS access may also remove a reason to add a second sender.'}, {'question': 'Does Drip support every SMS use case?', 'answer': 'No. Its current phased rollout has US paid-account and SAP conditions, requires an email for each phone record and provides one-way toll-free sending. Confirm current account access and required channel behavior.'}, {'question': 'What must a replacement preserve?', 'answer': 'Preserve event identity, tag meaning, branch rules, template fallbacks, channel permission, purchase exits and a single owner for queued messages. Demonstrate these before enabling sends.'}, {'question': 'Are the acceptance cases measured results?', 'answer': 'No. They are proposed fixtures for the merchant to execute. Record actual evidence and resolve failures before handing over queued steps.'}];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-16 md:py-24 container">
    <a href="/alternatives" class="link text-sm font-bold">← All alternatives</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Drip alternatives for cart recovery</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-xl text-slate-700">Decide whether to repair a Drip workflow or move it by mapping branches, events, tags and queued messages before changing senders.</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-10">
      <h2>Keep the workflow if the problem is repairable</h2>
      <p>Drip supports visual, behavior-triggered email workflows and segmentation. Keeping a working customer model can avoid rebuilding branches and retraining operators. A missed purchase exit or incorrect tag does not by itself show that the platform must be replaced. Trace one affected contact from entry event through each decision before buying another sender. See <a href="https://www.drip.com/ai-info">Drip’s product description</a> (checked November 25, 2026).</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.drip.com/sms">current SMS rollout</a> combines email, delays, decisions and SMS in one flow. Access is phased for existing US customers on paid plans with Support All People enabled; phone records require email, and SMS-only subscribers are unsupported. Launch scope is toll-free, one-way sending, without keyword-to-join, short codes or two-way replies. Confirm account access rather than assuming either universal availability or no native SMS.</p>
      <p>For an eligible merchant, adding native SMS may preserve useful workflow context. A business requiring replies, SMS-only identities or other regions has a concrete reason to evaluate another provider. A simpler email operation may instead value fewer branches and less maintenance. Write that requirement down so the replacement demonstration addresses the actual gap.</p>
      <h2>Make a branch handover record</h2>
      <p>For each live recovery workflow, record its entry event and producer, cart or checkout identifier, contact identifier, branch conditions, tag writers, delay steps, templates and exit rules. Save representative event payloads without customer secrets. Distinguish a tag used as an entry trigger from a tag used only for reporting; importing both as triggers can re-enroll old contacts.</p>
      <p>Map each branch to a destination rule and an owner. A repeat buyer, a first-time visitor and a contact awaiting a support answer may need different messages. Specify how missing properties behave: an unknown purchase status should not silently qualify for a discount. Document whether decisions read current state or the state at enrollment.</p>
      <p>Template migration needs more than screenshots. Rebuild product links, variant names, cart destinations, fallback text, sender details and unsubscribe behavior. Preview an empty cart and a deleted variant. Keep approved creative and explain which dynamic fields cannot be reproduced rather than substituting a broken generic link.</p>
      <h2>Choose a replacement demonstration</h2>
      <p>Use <a href="/alternatives/klaviyo-alternative">Klaviyo</a> or <a href="/alternatives/omnisend-alternative">Omnisend</a> as research paths for a replacement ecommerce workflow. Ask each candidate to reproduce your event and branch record, show exclusions before send, and expose a contact history an operator can explain. These links are starting points, not a measured ranking.</p>
      <p>If the missing requirement is a dedicated SMS operation, include <a href="/alternatives/postscript-alternative">Postscript</a> or <a href="/alternatives/recart-alternative">Recart</a> in procurement. Request proof of the required region, identity type, reply handling and opt-out propagation. Evaluate a Sequenzy lifecycle proposal through the <a href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-klaviyo">comparison</a> only if its demonstrated workflow reduces your maintenance burden; an additional layer is optional and adds coordination cost.</p>
      <p>Compare total contact and send charges, channel fees, integration work and ongoing branch maintenance using the same contact population. A lower subscription is useful only if the required events and exclusions remain maintainable. Keep Drip in the comparison when a repaired flow satisfies the requirement.</p>
      <h2>Exercise merchant cases before enabling sends</h2>
      <p>These are proposed acceptance fixtures, not tests performed for this article. For mobile distraction, restore the correct cart without a code and verify it works on the intended device. For a financing interruption, link to accurate payment information without promising approval. For shipping shock, show the actual destination-dependent delivery terms; an unrelated discount may leave the objection unresolved.</p>
      <p>For an unavailable variant, suppress the unavailable product or route to an explicitly approved alternative. For gift sizing, provide a size guide or staffed help route. For a minimum-order block, explain the quantity rule rather than sending a coupon that cannot bypass it. Keep each case tied to an event field, branch, template and expected exit so operators can diagnose failures.</p>
      <p>At an illustrative $407 order value and 45% gross margin, goods cost stays $223.85. Full-price gross profit is $183.15. A 20% discount reduces revenue to $325.60 and gross profit to $101.75, an $81.40 reduction before payment, fulfillment, messaging and returns. Check stacked coupons and repeat-purchaser eligibility before offering that reduction. Compare incremental profit against a comparable untreated cohort if volume supports one; attributed orders alone do not establish lift.</p>
      <h2>Hand over queued steps and retain rollback</h2>
      <p>Freeze workflow edits and inventory people waiting at each delay. Decide whether each queued step will drain in Drip or be canceled and recreated once. Do not import queued people as fresh abandoners by default. Separate historical contact migration from new event enrollment and agree which sender owns recovery during the boundary.</p>
      <p>Propose fixtures for a purchase arriving during a delay, an unsubscribe arriving after enrollment, a duplicate event and a replay of an older cart after a newer purchase. Expected behavior is no disallowed send, no duplicate enrollment and a traceable exit or suppression reason. Measure integration latency in the actual setup and design the delay around it; no universal fifteen-minute exit target or fixed touch cap establishes readiness.</p>
      <p>Retain workflow versions, exported permission scope, exclusions and a queue reconciliation record. If an exit or replay fixture fails, stop new destination enrollments, account for queued destination sends and restore one verified owner. Do not restart both systems. Release only after the responsible operator can explain each branch and account for the queued population; no fixed migration duration substitutes for that record.</p>
    </div>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before changing recovery tools" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
